for this week's classic interview we're  going to go back to when I spoke to  Christopher Petrella back in February of  2014 and this relates to what we often  talk about is a sort of two-tiered  justice system and very often when we  talk about that two-tier justice justice  system we're talking about those people  that are sort of oppressed by that  system we're talking about the  disproportionately poor the  disproportionately non-white  disproportionately people who are in  situations of oppression rather than  privilege and one of the things that is  a necessary analysis to what we saw in  terms of the Moller report and the  subsequent bar report is that the entire  way that Donald Trump was able to sort  of manage the Moller probe including  choosing his own Attorney General who  ultimately was given the opportunity by  Muller to adjudicate whether Donald from  theatre didn't commit obstruction of  justice this is the other side of the  two-tiered justice system appointing  your own Attorney General to adjudicate  your possible crimes is a position of  privilege on the complete other side of  the justice system and when I reviewed  this interview we did with Christopher  Petrella a few years ago that was the  sort of context I was thinking of let's  get into it from February of 2014  Christopher Petrella his research on a  lot of these issues related to the  justice system incarceration privilege  oppression etc Christopher Petrelli is a  PhD student in UC Berkeley's Department  of African American Studies his research  focuses on race class incarceration he's  also a featured blogger at nation of  change Chris you sent me these notes  about what you see as a connection  that's worth discussing between Hyper  incarceration in the United States which  is a topic we talk about a lot on this  program and Occupy Wall Street tell me  what do you have in mind here for our  audience sure well I think the way it  might be helpful to begin would be to  think about what Professor David Harvey  at the City University of New York  states the government does that the  state does during moments of economic  crisis and the term he uses  is accumulation by dispossession which  basically means divesting citizens of  rights and assets in order to at least  as it's justified in order to  restabilized or bring equilibrium back  to the economy and what we see now is  accumulation by dispossession being  being operationalized in two ways one is  through personal indebtedness and think  for example you know structural  readjustment policy on sort of an  individual register and a fine instance  with this would be the fact that  graduates of colleges and universities  right now or coming out with the student  debt law student debt load on average of  about twenty four thousand dollars a  year that's just for a bachelor's degree  never mind you know a master's or  anything beyond that and the second  strategy is to redistribute or  externalize costs from the state body to  who to this to citizens and we so we see  this happening both in public private  and public higher education that's being  privatized and publicly owned and  operated prison facilities that are also  being privatized and if I could just a  quick example we know for example I'm a  PhD student at UC Berkeley we know for  example that the Board of Regents are  proposing around an 81 percent fee  increase and at Berkeley fee is the  euphemistic term we use for tuition an  81 percent tuition increase over the  next four years at a time that the state  of stage facing budget crisis crisis  which means that we as taxpayers in the  state are actually enduring a form of  double taxation we're paying for our  education in the form of taxes and other  social services of course but then were  you know being taxed on top of that with  this fee increase and then in prisons  for example just last month the  Riverside California County of excuse me  Board of Directors at the riverside  county level decided that  we're going to begin charging prisoners  100 $42.42 per day for their stay so  it's a pay to pay program and you see  again the burden the cost being shifted  from the state's back to the most  vulnerable who don't have the money to  begin with anyway yeah we did that story  about the prisons the California prison  charging inmates and you know I think  it's interesting because they both will  eventually have the same undesired  opposite effect of maybe why they're  intended to be put in there I mean with  the prison example we were saying if you  do charge someone for being in prison  and when they leave prison they have  absolutely no money you're you're  creating a situation much more likely  for them to commit a crime that'll land  them back in prison in other words it's  actually having the opposite effect of  making someone no longer a criminal same  thing I think it could apply to the  overwhelming student loan debt burden  right in other words if people have this  incredible student loan debt they are  not necessarily in the criminal sense  but they're going to do things that they  wouldn't otherwise do if they were more  free in other words to kind of pursue  what they want to do so that is that  another similarity it is yeah I mean you  to speak on your first point we know for  example that in California  okay the state of California has the the  highest recidivism rate in the country  and recidivism is a term that basically  refers to the percentage of prisoners  after three years of release that end up  back in a penal institution and it  currently in California the number  stands at about 72 percent which is  lonesomely high and I agree with you you  know one of the very best strategies for  reducing the rate of recidivism would be  to make sure that inmates upon their  release aren't shouldering debt burdens  that are going to put undue strain on  their ability of course to repay or to  get a job for example so I agree and  then of course to your second point in  public education particularly at the the  the level of higher education public  higher education we see a lot of very  very bright students  a lot of students who are publicly  minded and want to work you know in the  realm of sort of public service more  generally finding that they you know  have very few options and then end up on  Wall Street for lack of better for lack  of a better option because they're  shouldering such a large debt load talk  a little bit about hyper incarceration  in the u.s. I mean the statistics are  very clear 5% of the world's population  is in the u.s. 25 percent of the world's  prisoners are in the u.s. now a very  superficial approach might say you know  if it wasn't if there weren't so many  nonviolent drug offenses that landed  people in jail that would solve a huge  portion of it but is that is that  realistic to think that how big of a  problem is the jailing of nonviolent  drug offenders nonviolent drug offenders  it's a it's a problem of excused upon  but capital proportions it's egregious I  mean I'll give you an example so I I'm  27 years old I was born in 1984 1984  since 1984 the the number of bodies  incarcerated in this country has  burgeoned by 400% at a time when the  population has grown ten times as slowly  the number of private prison facilities  since 1984 has grown by four thousand  percent you know there are now you know  ten about ten percent or so of prisons  are for-profit facilities or private  facilities contractor excuse me private  contracts given by the federal  government and we see a situation now  where in in private and public prisons  about 70 percent for example of people  of color are incarcerated for nonviolent  drug related offenses and there are  statistics on this and I'd like to share  one we found that if the state of  California for example emptied its  prisons and released every single inmate  the state and and sent all of these in  these former prisoners to  UC Berkeley UC Merced any of the UCS the  state would actually save about seven  billion dollars per year in other on  paying for their education would be much  cheaper than what it cost to have them  in prison indeed it's very clear I mean  the tuition at UC Berkeley right now  although rising at sort of a rates far  outstripping inflation is still far less  than it is then it costs to incarcerate  someone in a medium-security facility  for example I you know teach at a number  of courses at San Quentin and at San  Quentin the cost of incarcerating a  prisoner is about $47,000 that's in 2010  that's up from about $30,000 in 1990 and  we're talking you know David this is a  medium-security generally medium  security facility death row inmates at  San Quentin cost nearly $200,000 to  incarcerate per year so from a budget  from a budgetary perspective which is a  very slim sort of metric of course this  makes absolutely no sense  it's incredible Kristen there's so much  more to get into and unfortunately we're  just out of time but we'll absolutely  have to have you back  Chris Petrella is a PhD student at UC  Berkeley's Department of African  American Studies he's also a featured  blogger at nation of change really great  to talk to you Chris David thank you so  much

